course: dictionary
lesson: 6831
title: The sudden start of something bad
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: An outbreak is a word that often shows up in the news and in history books. It means the sudden start of something bad, such as a disease, a war or violence. «There have been outbreaks of measles in England in recent years.» The word sudden and the words about measles tell you that an illness has started to spread quickly.
explain: «London's main sewers helped end the city's outbreaks of cholera, a deadly illness spread by dirty water.» The clue is deadly illness. An outbreak here is a time when this dangerous illness suddenly spreads among many people. Try the test: put the word spread in place of outbreaks, and the sentence still makes sense.
explain: Words can also be about bigger events. «Hernán Cortés conquered Tenochtitlan in 1521, helped by a smallpox outbreak.» Smallpox is an illness, so an outbreak of smallpox is the sudden spread of that illness, and the sentence says it helped the Spanish win. You do not need to know every detail to see the meaning.
fact: Outbreak is a noun: one outbreak, several outbreaks.
fact: «An outbreak of measles» means many people suddenly catch measles in one place.
fact: «The outbreak of war» means the sudden start of a war.
fact: When you are ill, wash your hands and stay home, so that an outbreak does not spread to other people.
fact: Outbreak is a sudden start, so it is not a slow or gentle change.
exercise: «There have been outbreaks of measles in England in recent years.» What is an outbreak? | *The sudden start of something bad | A slow, gentle change | A long, quiet holiday
exercise: «London's main sewers helped end the city's outbreaks of cholera, a deadly illness spread by dirty water.» What does outbreaks of cholera mean? | A new kind of sewer tunnel | Clean water in the city | *Times when many people suddenly catch cholera
exercise: «Hernán Cortés conquered Tenochtitlan in 1521, helped by a smallpox outbreak.» What was spreading in this outbreak? | A treasure | *A disease | A river | A new city
test: The word outbreak is a: | verb | adjective | *noun
test: What does «an outbreak of food poisoning» mean? | A long walk | A new food shop | A quiet picnic | *Many people suddenly getting ill from food
exam: Why should you stay home when you are ill, to help stop an outbreak? | Because it is a holiday | Because stay-home days are fun | *So the illness does not spread to other people
